Automated Chinese bitmap font synchronization
==============================================

The firmware uses a project-specific `ChineseGFXfont` structure. It contains
standard `GFXglyph` bitmap data plus a uint16_t Unicode lookup table used by
`MyGxEPD2_BW`. The upstream Adafruit C converter does not generate this extra
lookup table, so Chinese fonts are generated by
`fontconvert/fontconvert_unicode.py`.

Windows one-click update:

  Double-click `update_fonts.cmd`.

The command scans the active locale and firmware string literals, adds the
characters in `extra_glyphs.txt`, regenerates the configured font sizes, checks
the 64 KiB bitmap-offset limit, replaces all outputs only after every size has
converted successfully, validates the written files, and builds the firmware.

Read-only missing-glyph check:

  powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File update_fonts.ps1 -Check

Update without compiling:

  powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File update_fonts.ps1 -Apply

Generate into a staging directory without touching project fonts:

  powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File update_fonts.ps1 -Apply -OutputDir C:\Temp\epd-fonts

Update and compile:

  powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File update_fonts.ps1 -Apply -Build

Direct Python equivalents:

  python update_fonts.py
  python update_fonts.py --apply
  python update_fonts.py --apply --build

Configuration is stored in `font_sync.json`. The default source font is
`C:/Windows/Fonts/Dengb.ttf`; set the `EPD_FONT_FILE` environment variable or
pass `--font` to use another TTF/OTF/TTC file. Do not commit a proprietary font
file unless its license permits redistribution.

Only the active `LOCALE` is scanned. Generated font headers and source comments
are excluded. Text returned dynamically by a weather service cannot be found by
static analysis, so place all possible runtime characters, alternate city
names, and planned UI vocabulary in `extra_glyphs.txt`.

The default sync target is the 16pt Dengb font used by setup and error pages.
Additional sizes can be added to the `sizes` array in `font_sync.json`, or
selected for one run with `-Sizes 12,16,26` in PowerShell.

Dependencies are listed in `requirements.txt`. The PowerShell wrapper installs
missing Python dependencies automatically unless `-NoInstall` is supplied.


Legacy Latin font generation
============================

Instructions for running ttf_to_adafruit_gfx.sh:

Dependencies:
  fonttools
    fonttools must be installed
    pyftsubset is part of fonttools, a python based command line tool for
    manipulating fonts. pyftsubset will be used to generate 2 subsets of the
    desired font.
  Adafruit fontconvert
    Adafruit provides a tool as part of their Adafruit GFX Library that converts
    font files to c-style arrays that can be used by the before-mentioned
    library. The necessary components have been included as part of this
    repository AND REMAIN LICENSED UNDER ITS ORIGINAL LICENSE AGREEMENT. A COPY
    OF THIS LICENSE IS PROVIDED ALONGSIDE THE SOURCE IN THE 'fontconvert'
    SUB-DIRECTORY OF THIS FOLDER.
    https://github.com/adafruit/Adafruit-GFX-Library/tree/master/fontconvert
  freetype2
    dependency for fontconvert.

How do I generate different sizes of fonts?
  In ttf_to_adafruit_gfx.sh there is an array called SIZES which specifies what
  size fonts should be generated.

How do I generate and add files for a new font?
  Add the .ttf or .otf file to the ./ttf directory then run the script.
  The script will generate a new subdirectory, ./fonts.
  Manually move the newly generated font fonts folder to
  platformio/lib/esp32-weather-epd-assets/fonts.

To run this script execute the following command:
  bash ttf_to_adafruit_gfx.sh

What does this script do?
  This script converts ttf or otf (font) files to c-style arrays to be used by
  the Adafruit GFX Library.

  2 font subsets will be generated:
    1. Extended ASCII (ISO-8859-1)
    2. Numbers (0-9), decimal point (.), dash (-), and the degree symbol (°)

  This is done to conserve EEPROM space.
  (only need 0..9/./-/° for the large primary temperature display)

The fonts used for this project could be swapped out relatively easily if
desired.
